1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,150
Shout down an empty hallway, and the sound travels.

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,559
Now fill the hallway with random junk.

3
00:00:05,559 --> 00:00:10,994
You would expect the sound to get through slowly, like someone pushing through a crowd.

4
00:00:10,994 --> 00:00:15,318
In 1958, the physicist Philip Anderson found something much stranger.

5
00:00:15,318 --> 00:00:19,456
Randomness alone can stop a wave completely, with no walls at all.

6
00:00:19,456 --> 00:00:25,200
Today we ask when that happens, and look at a new proof that nobody has fully confirmed yet.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,376
Here is a real simulation on a computer.

8
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:32,046
On top, a wave moves along a clean line and spreads far.

9
00:00:32,046 --> 00:00:34,815
Below, the same wave meets random heights.

10
00:00:34,815 --> 00:00:37,455
It bounces, and it meets its own echoes.

11
00:00:37,455 --> 00:00:40,610
Those echoes cancel each other, again and again.

12
00:00:40,610 --> 00:00:45,568
So the wave stays near where it started, even though nothing blocks the way.

13
00:00:45,568 --> 00:00:50,140
In our run, the clean wave reached a width of about eighty-five sites.

14
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:52,200
The messy one stayed near five.

15
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,298
Now, the shape of the world matters.

16
00:00:55,298 --> 00:00:57,658
A hallway gives a wave one route.

17
00:00:57,658 --> 00:00:59,254
A floor gives it many.

18
00:00:59,254 --> 00:01:01,405
A building gives it even more.

19
00:01:01,405 --> 00:01:04,944
With more routes, echoes find it harder to cancel.

20
00:01:04,944 --> 00:01:07,373
Physicists predicted this in 1979.

21
00:01:07,373 --> 00:01:09,871
In a line, any mess traps the wave.

22
00:01:09,871 --> 00:01:14,035
In three dimensions, a little mess should leave waves free.

23
00:01:14,035 --> 00:01:22,640
Our small simulation shows that trend, but be careful: these grids are tiny, so it illustrates the idea and proves nothing.

24
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,727
Physicists believed it.

25
00:01:24,727 --> 00:01:26,991
Mathematicians could not prove it.

26
00:01:26,991 --> 00:01:32,488
The trapped side came first: in 1980 for a line, and later for very strong disorder.

27
00:01:32,488 --> 00:01:36,951
But the free side, on a real three-dimensional grid, kept resisting.

28
00:01:36,951 --> 00:01:43,935
In the year 2000, the mathematician Barry Simon made it problem number one on his list for the new century.

29
00:01:43,935 --> 00:01:50,920
Prove that in three dimensions or more, a little disorder leaves a range of energies where waves stay free.

30
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:52,286
Why so hard?

31
00:01:52,286 --> 00:01:55,862
Because the grid is infinite and every point is random.

32
00:01:55,862 --> 00:01:59,758
Somewhere, there is always a rare region that behaves badly.

33
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:02,312
A proof cannot just handle the average.

34
00:02:02,312 --> 00:02:06,846
It has to control every trouble spot at once, at every scale, forever.

35
00:02:06,846 --> 00:02:13,040
It is like promising that a city's traffic will always flow, when any street could suddenly jam.

36
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:17,541
In September 2026, OpenAI released a paper claiming a proof.

37
00:02:17,541 --> 00:02:19,687
Here is the plan in plain words.

38
00:02:19,687 --> 00:02:21,183
Work on a finite grid.

39
00:02:21,183 --> 00:02:28,335
Reveal the randomness one binary digit at a time, and keep strict accounts of how the wave quantities change.

40
00:02:28,335 --> 00:02:34,317
If a local step goes wrong, undo it and mark that block as pending, to be cleaned up later.

41
00:02:34,317 --> 00:02:42,120
In the end, the accounts turn into a statement about the infinite grid: on a certain range of energies, waves are free.

42
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:45,747
It is important to say what this does not claim.

43
00:02:45,747 --> 00:02:50,035
It does not find the exact boundary between trapped and free waves.

44
00:02:50,035 --> 00:02:55,331
It does not settle ordinary diffusion, which is a separate problem on Simon's list.

45
00:02:55,331 --> 00:03:01,763
And in three dimensions, it only shows that a small enough disorder exists, without saying how small.

46
00:03:01,763 --> 00:03:06,240
So even if it is right, this is one big step, and not the whole story.

47
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:12,559
A researcher named Yao Xu turned this into Lean, a program that checks logic step by step.

48
00:03:12,559 --> 00:03:15,355
So we ran it ourselves, on a clean machine.

49
00:03:15,355 --> 00:03:20,058
It built with no errors, and it relies only on the three standard axioms.

50
00:03:20,058 --> 00:03:21,519
That is real evidence.

51
00:03:21,519 --> 00:03:23,617
But a computer check has limits.

52
00:03:23,617 --> 00:03:25,523
It does not explain the idea.

53
00:03:25,523 --> 00:03:31,560
And people still have to confirm that the code asks the same question as the original problem.

54
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:33,750
So where do things stand?

55
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:38,329
MathDB still lists the problem as open, and calls the claim unverified.

56
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:41,636
We know of no independent review by mathematicians.

57
00:03:41,636 --> 00:03:45,960
We read the introduction and the plan of the paper, not every page.

58
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:52,320
So please treat this as a striking claim backed by a machine check, and not yet as settled science.

59
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,802
Here is the idea in one breath.

60
00:03:54,802 --> 00:03:59,660
A wave in a messy world can be trapped by its own echoes, with no walls at all.

61
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:01,603
In a line, that always happens.

62
00:04:01,603 --> 00:04:09,437
In three dimensions, a little mess should leave waves free, and now there is a claimed proof, waiting for experts to confirm it.

63
00:04:09,437 --> 00:04:13,323
All the mathematics belongs to the people named in the sources.

64
00:04:13,323 --> 00:04:16,177
This explanation was made with the help of AI.

65
00:04:16,177 --> 00:04:18,120
Sources are in the description.
